    q: "A 55-year-old man presents with sudden-onset 'thunderclap' headache — the worst headache of his life. He has no fever, neck stiffness, or focal neuro deficits. CT head is negative. What is the next step?",
    a: "<b>Diagnosis to rule out:</b> <b>Subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH)</b><br><b>CT head sensitivity:</b> ~98% within 6 hours of onset, drops to ~85–90% at 24 hours<br><b>Next step after negative CT:</b> <b>Lumbar puncture</b><ul><li>Look for <b>xanthochromia</b> (yellow CSF from RBC breakdown — develops 2–4 hours after bleed, persists up to 2 weeks)</li><li>Or &gt;100,000 RBCs that do NOT clear between tubes 1 and 4</li></ul><b>If LP positive → CT angiography</b> to identify aneurysm<br><b>Most common cause:</b> <b>ruptured Berry aneurysm</b> (at Circle of Willis bifurcations)<br><b>Management:</b> nimodipine (prevents vasospasm), neurosurgical evaluation (clipping or coiling), avoid hypotension."
