    q: "A 48-year-old obese woman presents with severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, nausea, and vomiting after eating a fatty meal. Lipase is 1,850 U/L. What scoring system predicts severity, and what is the initial management?",
    a: "<b>Diagnosis:</b> <b>Acute pancreatitis</b> (most common cause: <b>gallstones</b> in women; alcohol in men)<br><b>Severity scoring (Ranson's criteria):</b><ul><li><b>On admission:</b> Age &gt;55, WBC &gt;16K, glucose &gt;200, LDH &gt;350, AST &gt;250</li><li><b>At 48h:</b> BUN rise &gt;5, Ca &lt;8, PaO2 &lt;60, base deficit &gt;4, fluid sequestration &gt;6L, Hct drop &gt;10%</li><li>≥3 criteria = severe</li></ul><b>Initial management (IVF, NPO, pain control):</b><ol><li>Aggressive IV crystalloid resuscitation (<b>Lactated Ringer's preferred</b>)</li><li>Pain control (IV morphine or hydromorphone is acceptable)</li><li>NPO initially; restart oral feeds when pain improves and appetite returns</li><li><b>Do NOT give antibiotics</b> prophylactically; only if infected necrosis suspected</li></ol>"
