    a: "<b>Diagnosis:</b> <b>Acute Chest Syndrome (ACS)</b> — most common cause of death in sickle cell disease<br><b>Definition:</b> new pulmonary infiltrate + respiratory symptoms (chest pain, cough, fever, hypoxia) in SCD patient<br><b>Triggers:</b> infection, fat embolism from infarcted bone marrow, in situ sickling<br><b>Management:</b><ol><li>Supplemental oxygen to maintain SpO2 &gt; 95%</li><li><b>Simple transfusion</b> (Hgb 6–9 → target 10 g/dL) or <b>exchange transfusion</b> if severe</li><li>IV fluids (avoid overhydration)</li><li>Empiric antibiotics covering atypicals (azithromycin + cephalosporin)</li><li>Incentive spirometry, bronchodilators</li><li>Analgesia (opioids for pain — but avoid oversedation/hypoventilation)</li></ol><b>Prevention:</b> hydroxyurea (increases HbF), pneumococcal/flu vaccines."
