    q: "A 20-year-old college athlete presents with sudden onset of right-sided chest pain and dyspnea after coughing. He is tall and thin. O2 sat is 95%. CXR shows a thin pleural line in the right upper lobe with no lung markings beyond it. Trachea is midline. What is the management?",
    a: "<b>Diagnosis:</b> <b>Primary spontaneous pneumothorax (PSP)</b> — typical in tall, thin young males (ruptured subpleural blebs)<br><b>Management based on size:</b><ul><li><b>Small (&lt;2 cm rim) + stable:</b> supplemental O2 + observation for 3–6 hours; discharge with follow-up CXR in 24–48h</li><li><b>Large (&gt;2 cm rim) or symptomatic:</b> <b>needle aspiration</b> (14–16G catheter in 2nd ICS, midclavicular line) or chest tube (4th–5th ICS, midaxillary line)</li></ul><b>Tension pneumothorax clues:</b> tracheal deviation away from affected side, hypotension, absent breath sounds — <b>immediate needle decompression</b> without waiting for imaging<br><b>Recurrence:</b> 25–50% → consider video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) with pleurodesis after second episode."
