    a: "<b>Diagnosis:</b> <b>Subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH)</b> from posterior communicating artery (PComm) aneurysm (CN III palsy = compression of superficial pupillomotor fibers)<br><b>Feared complications:</b><ol><li><b>Rebleeding</b> — highest risk in first 24 hours; treated by surgical clipping or endovascular coiling</li><li><b>Vasospasm</b> — peaks at days 4–14; causes delayed ischemic deficits<ul><li>Prevented with: <b>Nimodipine</b> 60 mg q4h × 21 days (oral calcium channel blocker)</li></ul></li><li><b>Hydrocephalus</b> — CSF obstruction → ventriculostomy</li><li><b>Hyponatremia</b> — from SIADH or cerebral salt wasting</li></ol><b>Blood pressure control:</b> target SBP &lt; 160 until aneurysm secured; avoid hypotension."
