    q: "A 55-year-old hypothyroid woman is brought in after being found unresponsive at home in winter. T is 34°C, HR 48, BP 85/50, RR 10. TSH is >100. She is on levothyroxine but ran out. What is the diagnosis and management?",
    a: "<b>Diagnosis:</b> <b>Myxedema coma</b> (severe hypothyroidism decompensated by cold, illness, or medication noncompliance)<br><b>Cardinal features:</b><ul><li>Hypothermia, bradycardia, hypotension, hyporeflexia</li><li>Altered mental status → coma</li><li>Hypoventilation (CO2 retention), hyponatremia, hypoglycemia</li><li>Periorbital edema, coarse skin, macroglossia</li></ul><b>Management (ICU):</b><ol><li><b>IV levothyroxine (T4)</b> 200–400 mcg loading dose, then 50–100 mcg/day</li><li>Add <b>IV liothyronine (T3)</b> 5–20 mcg q8h (faster acting)</li><li><b>IV hydrocortisone</b> 100 mg q8h (rule out concurrent adrenal insufficiency — give steroids before thyroid hormone to prevent adrenal crisis)</li><li>Passive rewarming, glucose, mechanical ventilation if needed</li></ol>"
